                        <div class="mm-mod">Agent<small>preview</small></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="mm-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&#8595;</div>
                <div class="mm-wizard">A setup wizard opens in your browser and walks the rest.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <p style="margin-top: 1.75rem;">That wizard is four short steps &mdash; the same on every path:</p>

        <p style="margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
            <span class="status-badge status-done">Account</span> <span style="color: var(--fg-dim);">&rarr;</span>
            <span class="status-badge status-done">Vault</span> <span style="color: var(--fg-dim);">&rarr;</span>
            <span class="status-badge status-done">Expose</span> <span style="color: var(--fg-dim);">&rarr;</span>
            <span class="status-badge status-done">Done</span>
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li><strong>Account.</strong> Create your admin login. On a server you paste a one-time bootstrap token first, then choose your own username + password &mdash; in the browser, never in a config file.</li>
            <li><strong>Vault.</strong> Create your first vault, or import one from a git repo. A fresh vault opens with a <strong>Getting Started</strong> note &mdash; a short guide your AI reads to help shape the vault around how you actually think and work.</li>
            <li><strong>Expose.</strong> Choose how (or whether) to reach your hub from outside. Auto-skips on a laptop; pre-selects public on a server so the wizard URL is reachable.</li>
            <li><strong>Done.</strong> The wizard hands you everything you need to connect your AI.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    {# ============ MOVEMENT 3 · PICK WHERE IT RUNS ============ #}
    <section class="section">
        <h2>Pick where it runs</h2>

        <p>Two homes, one stack. An always-on <strong>Linux box</strong> your phone and your AIs can reach around the clock, or your own <strong>Mac</strong> to try it in a minute. Nothing's locked in &mdash; start on the laptop and move to a box whenever you like.</p>

        <div class="where-grid">

            {# ---- LINUX / ANY VPS ---- #}
            <div class="where-card">
                <h3>Any Linux box &mdash; always-on</h3>
                <p>Any fresh <strong>Ubuntu or Debian</strong> box works the same way: <strong>Hetzner</strong> (x86 or arm64), <strong>EC2</strong>, <strong>DigitalOcean</strong>, or an Ubuntu machine in your closet. Create it, open a root shell, and paste one line:</p>

                <div class="code-inline">
<code>curl -fsSL https://parachute.computer/install/server.sh | bash</code>
                </div>

                <p>It self-installs hub + vault, fetches a real Let's Encrypt cert via <a href="https://caddyserver.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Caddy</a>, and prints your <strong>bootstrap token</strong> and <strong>setup URL</strong> right there. Open the URL, set your username + password in the browser &mdash; you're the owner. No SSH key to wrangle, and <strong>no domain needed</strong>: you get working HTTPS at <code>https://&lt;your-ip&gt;.sslip.io</code> automatically.</p>

                <p class="muted"><strong>Firewall:</strong> open ports <strong>80</strong> and <strong>443</strong> &mdash; a <em>Security Group</em> on EC2, a <em>Cloud Firewall</em> on Hetzner. DigitalOcean droplets and closet boxes need nothing.</p>

                <p class="muted"><strong>apt-based only.</strong> RPM distros (Amazon Linux, RHEL) aren't supported yet.</p>

                <details style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                    <summary>DigitalOcean, click by click</summary>
                    <ol style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                        <li><strong>Create an Ubuntu 24.04 droplet.</strong> In the DigitalOcean console (<a href="https://cloud.digitalocean.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">cloud.digitalocean.com</a>) &rarr; <strong>Create &rarr; Droplets</strong>: image <strong>Ubuntu 24.04 (LTS) x64</strong>, size <strong>$6/mo (1 GB)</strong> for hub + vault (pick $12/mo / 2 GB for on-box transcription or to run Claude Code on the box), region nearest you. An SSH key is optional &mdash; you won't need it.</li>
                        <li><strong>Open the droplet's Console.</strong> On its page click <strong>Access &rarr; Launch Droplet Console</strong> &mdash; a root terminal right in your browser. (If it prompts for a password, set one on that Access tab first.)</li>
                        <li><strong>Paste the one-liner above and run it.</strong></li>
                        <li><strong>Wait ~2&ndash;3 minutes.</strong> It prints your <strong>bootstrap token</strong> (<code>parachute-bootstrap-&hellip;</code>) and <strong>setup URL</strong> in the Console.</li>
                        <li><strong>Open that URL</strong> &mdash; it lands on <code>/admin/setup</code>. Paste the token, choose your username + password. Done.</li>
                    </ol>
                    <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">The token is single-use and regenerates if the hub restarts; re-read the current one any time with <code>cat parachute-setup.txt</code>.</p>
                </details>

                <details style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
                    <summary>Use your own domain</summary>
                    <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">First point it at the box &mdash; in your DNS provider add an <strong>A record</strong> for the hostname you want (e.g. <code>vault.example.com</code>) &rarr; your box's public IPv4. Then run it with the domain set:</p>
                    <div class="code-inline">
<code>PARACHUTE_DOMAIN=vault.example.com curl -fsSL https://parachute.computer/install/server.sh | bash</code>
                    </div>
                    <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">Caddy fetches a Let's Encrypt cert for it automatically. Already running and want to switch addresses later? On the box, <code>parachute hub set-origin https://vault.example.com</code> rewrites Caddy, fetches a fresh cert, and restarts your modules &mdash; your data and account are untouched. Afterward, re-point your AI / MCP clients at the new URL.</p>
                </details>

                <details style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
                    <summary>Prefer unattended / Infrastructure-as-Code?</summary>
                    <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">Paste the <em>same</em> one-liner into the box's <strong>cloud-init / User Data</strong> at create time, prefixed with a <code>#!/bin/bash</code> line &mdash; it self-installs on first boot, no Console step:</p>
                    <div class="code-inline">
<code>#!/bin/bash<br>
curl -fsSL https://parachute.computer/install/server.sh | bash</code>
                    </div>
                    <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">Then grab your bootstrap token once it's up: <code>ssh root@&lt;your-ip&gt; cat parachute-setup.txt</code> (or open the Console and <code>cat parachute-setup.txt</code>). For a domain in this path, add an <code>export PARACHUTE_DOMAIN=vault.example.com</code> line above the <code>curl</code>.</p>
                </details>
            </div>

            {# ---- MAC ---- #}
            <div class="where-card">
                <h3>Your Mac &mdash; try it in a minute</h3>
                <p>Runs on your own machine under <strong>launchd</strong>, free, in about a minute. The catch: it sleeps when your Mac sleeps, so your phone and your AIs can't reach it while it's closed &mdash; perfect for kicking the tires. You'll need <a href="https://bun.sh" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bun</a> 1.3 or later, then one command:</p>

                <div class="code-inline">
<code>bun add -g @openparachute/hub &amp;&amp; parachute init</code>
                </div>

                <p><code>parachute init</code> starts your hub, installs your vault, and opens the browser setup wizard. On a laptop the <strong>Expose</strong> step auto-skips &mdash; you're signed in locally. Want it reachable around the clock later? Move to an always-on Linux box &mdash; same stack, nothing's locked in.</p>

                <details style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                    <summary>Checking your Bun version</summary>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                        <li><strong>Check it.</strong> <code>bun --version</code> &mdash; if it's below 1.3, run <code>bun upgrade</code>.</li>
                        <li><strong>Stuck on 1.2.x?</strong> You may see a cosmetic <code>InvalidPackageResolution</code> or <code>FileNotFound</code> warning during install &mdash; the package installs anyway. Upgrading to Bun 1.3 clears it.</li>
                    </ul>
                </details>

                <details style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
                    <summary>Ports, and re-running</summary>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                        <li><strong>Ports.</strong> The hub answers on <code>1939</code>; the vault runs on <code>1940</code> behind it.</li>
                        <li><strong>Safe to re-run.</strong> <code>init</code> is idempotent. Check the stack any time with <code>parachute status</code>.</li>
                    </ul>
                </details>
            </div>

        </div>
    </section>

    {# ============ MOVEMENT 4 · GROW ============ #}
    <section class="section">
        <h2>Grow from here</h2>

        <p>Your hub is up and you're signed in. Three moves open the rest: reach it from anywhere, connect your AI, and build a surface of your own over your vault.</p>

        <h3>Reach it from anywhere</h3>
        <p>The Linux one-liner already did this &mdash; Caddy-direct with a real Let's Encrypt cert on your own HTTPS address, no tunnel. On a <strong>Mac</strong> or any box you set up by hand, <code>parachute expose public</code> gives you a public URL: add <code>--cloudflare --domain vault.example.com</code> for a <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/products/tunnel/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloudflare Tunnel</a> on your own domain (the apex must already be a Cloudflare zone; the free tier is fine, and <code>expose</code> <em>creates</em> the tunnel for you). Drop <code>--cloudflare</code> for a domain-free <code>*.ts.net</code> URL over <a href="https://tailscale.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tailscale</a> Funnel; or <code>tailscale serve</code> keeps the hub private to your own tailnet.</p>

        <h3 id="connect-mcp-clients">Connect your AI</h3>
        <p>Connecting any AI &mdash; Claude.ai on the web, ChatGPT, Claude Code, Cursor, Zed, your own agent &mdash; comes down to one URL: your vault's MCP endpoint. Point your client at it (web UIs call this a <strong>connector</strong>):</p>

        <div class="code-inline">
<code>&lt;hub-origin&gt;/vault/&lt;name&gt;/mcp</code>
        </div>

        <p><code>&lt;hub-origin&gt;</code> is the address your hub answers on &mdash; the same URL you sign in at, <em>not</em> the vault's own port <code>1940</code>. <code>&lt;name&gt;</code> is your vault name (<code>default</code> unless you changed it). The <strong><code>/mcp</code> suffix is required</strong>. OAuth-capable clients send you through hub sign-in automatically.</p>

        <details open>
            <summary>Claude.ai, ChatGPT &amp; other web AIs</summary>
            <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">In your AI's settings, add a new <strong>connector</strong> (custom MCP server) pointing at <code>&lt;hub-origin&gt;/vault/&lt;name&gt;/mcp</code>. You'll be sent to your hub to sign in and approve &mdash; then the AI can read and write your vault. Nothing to install; the easiest path for most people.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>Claude Code &amp; other CLI / MCP clients</summary>
            <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">Register the server once with <code>claude mcp add</code> &mdash; the wizard's done screen gives you a ready-to-paste command. The first connection sends you through your hub's sign-in in the browser (OAuth); after that, start a new session and run <code>/mcp</code> &mdash; the vault's tools are there. Codex, Goose, Cursor, and Zed take the same <code>&lt;hub-origin&gt;/vault/&lt;name&gt;/mcp</code> URL. (Headless box that can't do the browser flow? Mint a token on the vault's Tokens page and append <code>--header "Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;"</code>.)</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>Clients that need a bearer token</summary>
            <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">Some clients can't do OAuth and want a static token instead. Mint one from the vault admin's <strong>Tokens</strong> page, then give it to the client alongside the same <code>&lt;hub-origin&gt;/vault/&lt;name&gt;/mcp</code> URL.</p>
        </details>

        <h3>See your first note &mdash; then build a surface</h3>
        <p>Here's the payoff. Ask your AI to jot a quick note (&ldquo;jot down that I'm trying Parachute today&rdquo;), then look at it. <strong>Nothing to install:</strong> open <a href="https://notes.parachute.computer" target="_blank" rel="noopener">notes.parachute.computer</a>, the hosted Notes app, enter your hub's vault URL (<code>&lt;hub-origin&gt;/vault/&lt;name&gt;</code>) and sign in. The app runs in your browser; your notes never leave your hub.</p>

        <p>When you're ready for something shaped to <em>your</em> brain &mdash; a dashboard, a daily journal, a garden &mdash; build a <strong>surface</strong>: a custom UI over your vault, in your own codebase, no Parachute install needed. <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@openparachute/surface-client" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><code>@openparachute/surface-client</code></a> gives you OAuth sign-in + the vault API and <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@openparachute/surface-render" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><code>surface-render</code></a> renders your notes &mdash; a working surface in a few lines, hosted free on GitHub Pages.</p>

        <figure style="margin: 1.5rem 0 0;">
            <div class="surface-shot-frame">
                <img src="/assets/install/unisurface.png" alt="A custom Parachute surface over a personal vault, with the quick-capture panel open and the background dimmed behind it" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="1000">
            </div>
            <figcaption class="surface-shot-cap">A personal surface &mdash; &ldquo;a quiet garden for the mind&rdquo;: a custom UI built on top of one's own vault.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="starter-callout" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3>Keep climbing: Vault &rarr; Surface &rarr; Agent</h3>
            <p>Your vault is the core. A <strong>surface</strong> is a UI shaped to how you think. An <strong>agent</strong> acts on the vault for you. Your vault's <strong>Getting Started</strong> note walks your AI through filling it and building your first surface end to end &mdash; just ask your connected AI to <em>&ldquo;read the Getting Started note and help me set up my parachute.&rdquo;</em></p>
            <p>See how the pieces compose in the <a href="/modules/">modules overview</a>, build your own UI with the <a href="/surfaces/">surface guide</a>, and for power moves, <a href="/scripting/">script your vault</a> from bash, Python, or JS &mdash; or host for friends and family with the <a href="/multi-user/">multi-user operator guide</a>.</p>
        </div>

        <p style="margin-top: 1.5rem;"><strong>Staying current:</strong> <code>parachute upgrade</code> sweeps the hub and every installed service to the latest release (add <code>--channel rc</code> to track release candidates). Vault migrations run automatically on first post-upgrade boot. Back up your whole vault to portable markdown with <code>parachute-vault export &lt;dir&gt;</code> (and <code>import</code> back).</p>
    </section>

    {# ============ TROUBLESHOOTING (preserved) ============ #}
    <section class="section">
        <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>

        <details>
            <summary>Something not working?</summary>
            <ul style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                <li><strong>Claude Code doesn't see the vault.</strong> Start a new session (MCP config loads at session start) and run <code>/mcp</code>. Still missing? Re-run <code>parachute-vault mcp-install</code> on the hub machine.</li>
                <li><strong>Port 1940 already in use.</strong> Run <code>lsof -i :1940</code> to see what's there; change ports in <code>~/.parachute/vault/.env</code>.</li>
                <li><strong>A client can't reach the MCP endpoint.</strong> Confirm the URL ends in <code>/mcp</code> and points at the <em>hub</em> origin (the address you sign in at), not the vault's own port <code>1940</code>.</li>
                <li><strong>Stuck on Bun 1.2.x?</strong> You may see a cosmetic <code>InvalidPackageResolution</code> or <code>FileNotFound</code> warning &mdash; the package installs anyway. Upgrading to Bun 1.3 clears it.</li>
                <li>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Claude connector fails right after consent over a Cloudflare tunnel</summary>
                        <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">You exposed your hub publicly with a <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/products/tunnel/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloudflare Tunnel</a> (<code>parachute expose public --cloudflare</code>) and tried to add your vault to the <strong>Claude Desktop / claude.ai / mobile MCP connector</strong>. OAuth consent succeeds, then Claude reports <em>&ldquo;the integration rejected the credentials&rdquo;</em> or <em>&ldquo;the connection was reverted&rdquo;</em> and never reaches the vault &mdash; while Notes and Claude Code (which use a static token) keep working fine, so it looks like an OAuth bug.</p>
                        <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;"><strong>Cause &mdash; this is a Cloudflare edge setting, not a Parachute bug.</strong> Consent runs in your <em>browser</em>, so it passes Cloudflare. But the token exchange (<code>POST /oauth/token</code>) and the MCP calls are <em>server-to-server</em> requests from Anthropic's servers &mdash; and Cloudflare's <strong>Bot Fight Mode</strong> / <strong>Super Bot Fight Mode</strong> / <strong>Browser Integrity Check</strong> (or a Cloudflare Access policy) challenges them with a <code>403</code> &ldquo;Access denied&rdquo; page. Claude gets the 403 instead of a token.</p>
                        <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;"><strong>Fix &mdash; in your Cloudflare dashboard for the exposed zone:</strong></p>
                        <ul style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
                            <li>Turn <strong>off</strong> Bot Fight Mode / Super Bot Fight Mode (Security &rarr; Bots), <em>or</em> add a <strong>WAF Skip rule</strong> scoped to your exposed hostname (or the API paths <code>/oauth/*</code>, <code>/.well-known/*</code>, <code>/vault/*</code>) that skips Bot Fight Mode, Managed Rules, and the Browser Integrity Check.</li>
                            <li>If <strong>Browser Integrity Check</strong> is on, disable it (or skip it for those paths).</li>
                            <li>If you put the hostname behind <strong>Cloudflare Access (Zero Trust)</strong>, remove that policy &mdash; the hub does its own OAuth, and an outer Access gate blocks the API.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;"><strong>Confirm it's this.</strong> From any non-browser tool, hit your public origin with a non-browser user-agent &mdash; e.g. <code>curl -A python-requests https://&lt;your-host&gt;/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server</code>. If Cloudflare's <code>403</code> &ldquo;Access denied&rdquo; HTML page comes back (rather than a hub response) while a normal browser loads the same URL fine, that's the cause.</p>
                    </details>
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">Found something we missed? <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-vault/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open an issue</a>.</p>
        </details>
    </section>

